window.CRT_NARRATIVES = {"consumer_discretionary":"# Consumer Discretionary: where the capital went, 1999-2026\n\n*Companion data: `research/events_group2.json` (ids `cd-`). Series: XLY; XHB/ITB, CARZ, PEJ, XRT, FDN.*\n\n## Preamble, 1999-2005: bubble, war, housing, Detroit\n\nFour separate stories. Internet retail was the heart of the dot-com bust: the Nasdaq peaked at 5,048.62 on March 10 2000 and fell 78% by October 2002 while capital rotated into \"old economy\" consumer names. September 11 2001 shut markets for a week and crushed travel and hotels (New York occupancy below 40%, $15 billion of airline aid). The Fed's 1% rates created the 2003-2005 housing boom, in which builders roughly tripled before peaking in July 2005 and falling 24% by November. And Detroit's decline became a credit event when S&P cut GM and Ford to junk on May 5 2005.\n\n## 2006-2009: the housing bust and the auto bankruptcies\n\nHousing led the sector into the crisis: prices peaked in July 2006 and builder stocks fell more than 70% by 2008 (Toll -62%, Pulte -73% from the peak), with furniture and home improvement following. Specialty retail broke when Circuit City filed on November 10 2008 and liquidated in January. Autos were last: GM and Chrysler took $17.4 billion of federal aid in December 2008, Chrysler filed on April 30 2009 and GM on June 1 with $173 billion of debt. The policy response (the $8,000 homebuyer credit, Cash for Clunkers in July-August 2009) pulled demand forward and marked the lows; Ford, which avoided bankruptcy, became the recovery vehicle.\n\n## 2009-2016: the long discretionary bull market\n\nFrom the March 2009 low, consumer discretionary was the best-performing sector for most of the decade, and the inflows were about two things: cheap money (autos sold on sub-prime-style financing, builders recovered as rates stayed at zero, with the real housing recovery arriving in 2012) and the internet. Amazon's share of retail grew every year, and the sub-industry's premium multiple was increasingly a bet that Amazon could enter any category. Restaurants had their own growth cycle, and its end was single-name: Chipotle's E. coli and norovirus outbreaks of October-December 2015 cut revenue from $4.5 billion to $3.9 billion and net income from $476 million to $23 million the following year, a lesson in what food-safety risk does to a 40x multiple.\n\n## 2017-2019: Amazon versus everyone, then tariffs\n\n2017 was the \"retail apocalypse\": a record roughly 8,000 store-closure announcements and XRT down 8.6% through November 9 while the S&P 500 rose 15%. Amazon's June 16 2017 purchase of Whole Foods took $29 billion out of grocers and mass merchants in a day. Within specialty retail, money rotated to off-price, auto parts and home improvement, the categories Amazon could not easily take. The trade war then gave apparel a new driver: the August 1 2019 announcement of tariffs on the remaining $300 billion of Chinese goods (15% effective September 1 on $112 billion) accelerated sourcing shifts to Vietnam, the exposure that would matter in 2025.\n\n## 2020-2021: COVID's two rotations\n\nThe pandemic produced the sharpest sub-industry dispersion in the sector's history. Norwegian fell 26.7% on March 9 2020 alone, the three cruise lines lost about $54 billion (70%) of value in under two months and then raised tens of billions of emergency debt and equity, which is why their shares never regained 2019 levels. Mall apparel failed in a wave (J.Crew, Neiman Marcus, JCPenney in May 2020) as April retail sales fell a record 16.4%. The winners were the home (Home Depot's Q2 sales +23.4%, Lowe's stock +65% in a year), e-commerce and Tesla (+743% in 2020, S&P 500 entry December 21). The second rotation began on November 9 2020 with Pfizer's vaccine: cruise lines and casinos rallied, Amazon fell 5%. The January 2021 GameStop squeeze ($17 to $483 intraday, 140% short interest) was the retail-flow extreme of that liquidity regime.\n\n## 2022-2023: rates and the goods-to-services shift\n\n2022 undid the pandemic trade. Amazon fell 51%, its worst year since 2000, dropping below $1 trillion; Tesla fell 68%. Mortgage rates hit 21-year highs and ITB lost 28.9% and XHB 26.3% while the S&P 500 fell 18%. Retailers that had over-ordered goods (Target, Walmart, Gap) took margin shocks in May 2022, and spending moved to services and travel. 2023 then inverted the housing trade: with existing homes locked up by low in-place mortgages, builders captured transactions through rate buy-downs, XHB was up 30% and ITB 38% by autumn and both hit records on the December 2023 Fed pivot. The October 2023 GLP-1 scare (Walmart's \"buying less\" comment) briefly dragged restaurants, snacks and beer.\n\n## 2024-2025: brand fatigue, tariffs and the low-income consumer\n\nNike's worst day ever (June 28 2024, -20%, $28 billion) summarised apparel's shift from mega-brands to challengers (On, Hoka, Deckers) and off-price. The 2025 tariff regime then hit the sector harder than any other: 25% on imported vehicles and parts (March 26; GM -7%), the April 2 \"Liberation Day\" tariffs with 46% on Vietnam (Nike -10 to -13%; Lululemon, Deckers, Gap, American Eagle and Wayfair down up to 11%), the end of de minimis (China April 2, all countries August 29), and the OBBBA's termination of EV credits after September 30 2025, which produced a Q3 pull-forward and a Q4 cliff for Rivian, Lucid and the legacy EV programs. Dollar General's 32% collapse in August 2024 had already flagged a stretched low-income consumer, and by 2025 quick-service restaurants and discounters traded on that cohort's traffic.\n\n## 2026: tariff relief, then a stall\n\nThe Supreme Court's February 20 2026 decision that IEEPA does not authorise tariffs voided the reciprocal duties: consumer discretionary rose about 1% that day, Amazon, Etsy, Tapestry and Williams-Sonoma gained, apparel popped because roughly 60% of its applied tariffs were IEEPA-based, and the Court of International Trade ordered refunds on April 20. The administration replaced them with a 10% Section 122 global tariff from February 24 and new Section 301 duties from July, and Section 232 tariffs on autos and parts were untouched. Despite the relief, the sector has stalled: XLY set an all-time high of 123.75 on January 12 2026 and was down 2.7% year-to-date at the end of October (roughly flat by early November after a 6% October bounce) while consumer staples rose 17%. With Amazon and Tesla dominating the fund, that stall reflects scepticism about Amazon's AI capex, the post-credit EV cliff, and a defensive rotation away from a weakening low-income consumer.\n\n## What the sub-industries trade on today\n\n**Internet and direct retail.** Amazon trades on the AWS/AI capex-versus-retail-margin debate and logistics cost deflation; the marketplaces (Etsy, eBay) on the tariff regime, where the February 2026 IEEPA ruling and April refunds were a windfall, Section 122/301 duties are the new base case, and the end of de minimis helps domestic sellers against Temu and Shein. The Chinese platforms trade on Beijing's stimulus.\n\n**Autos and auto parts.** Affordability (auto-loan rates and the Fed path), the Section 232 25% vehicle and parts tariffs that survived the IEEPA ruling, the EV demand cliff after the September 30 2025 credit expiry, and Tesla's robotaxi/AI narrative, which has decoupled it from unit economics. Mexico-exposed GM carries the most tariff beta.\n\n**Homebuilders and home improvement.** The 30-year mortgage rate and the Fed's cutting cycle are the master variable; builders earn outsized share while existing inventory is locked in but give back margin through incentives. Lumber and tariff input costs, first-time buyer policy and the 2022-2023 lesson that scarce supply can trump rates are secondary; home improvement waits for turnover.\n\n**Travel, leisure and restaurants.** Cruise lines and hotels trade on record bookings against the 2020 debt and dilution, international travel and the dollar; casinos on Macau and Las Vegas. Restaurants trade on low-income traffic (the value war since 2024), wage inflation and, episodically, GLP-1 headlines, which returned with the November 2025 price cuts.\n\n**Apparel, footwear and luxury.** Tariff policy dominates: the 46% Vietnam rate is gone but 10% Section 122 and Section 301 duties remain and refunds are flowing. Beyond trade, the group trades on brand heat (challengers versus Nike), China luxury demand (weak since 2023) and wholesale-versus-DTC mix.\n\n**Specialty and off-price retail.** Off-price (TJX, Ross, Burlington) trades on trade-down, amplified by 2025-2026 consumer stress; auto parts on the ageing fleet; the rest on tariffs, mall traffic and card delinquencies. Import-heavy names (Five Below, Best Buy, Wayfair) were April 2025's biggest losers and February 2026's biggest winners.\n","consumer_staples":"# Consumer Staples: where the capital went, 1999-2026\n\n*Companion data: `research/events_group2.json` (ids `cs-`). Series: XLP; PBJ plus baskets for household/personal products and staples retail.*\n\n## Preamble, 1999-2005: from pariah to refuge\n\nStaples are what investors own when they are afraid, and 1999 showed the other side: money left slow-growing food, household and tobacco companies to chase internet stocks, and staples fell in a year the Nasdaq rose 85.6%. Tobacco was the extreme, de-rated to record lows by the 1998 Master Settlement and the July 14 2000 Engle verdict ($145 billion punitive, $74 billion attributed to Philip Morris USA). The reversal began in March 2000 (Nasdaq -2.6%, S&P 500 +2.4%) and staples held value through the 2000-2002 bear market. The period ended at peak confidence in branded pricing power: P&G's roughly $54 billion purchase of Gillette (January 2005, cleared September 30 2005) was the largest consumer-products deal to date.\n\n## 2006-2009: the crisis trade\n\nThe 2008 crash was staples' finest hour in relative terms. XLP was the best-performing sector fund in both halves of 2008, down only mid-single digits by October while the S&P 500 was down 35-40%, as investors crowded into non-cyclical earnings and dividends; Walmart grew revenue 7% to $401 billion as shoppers traded down and was one of the few mega-caps to rise. The flip side arrived immediately: in the 2009 risk rally staples lagged (down 9.7% versus the S&P's 8.9% by late March 2009). The pattern, outperform in the drawdown and lag in the recovery, is the sector's defining factor loading and has repeated in 2011, 2018, 2020, 2022 and 2026.\n\n## 2010-2016: the bond-proxy era\n\nWith rates at zero, staples were bought as bond substitutes. Dividend yield, low volatility and emerging-market consumer growth drove a five-year re-rating in which P&G, Coca-Cola and the food companies traded at premium multiples despite low single-digit growth, peaking in mid-2016 when the 10-year yield touched its lows. Financial engineering filled the growth gap: Berkshire and 3G's Heinz deal (2013) and the Kraft Heinz merger (2015) promised that zero-based budgeting could manufacture margin indefinitely, and the sub-industry's valuation came to depend on that promise.\n\n## 2017-2019: the de-rating\n\nThe promise broke on three fronts. Rates: XLP fell 2.1% in 2017 while SPY gained 15.4%, then dropped 13% by mid-May 2018 as the 10-year yield rose from 2.4% to 3.1%, on track for its worst year since 2008's 17% decline. Amazon: the June 16 2017 Whole Foods deal took 9.2% off Kroger, 7.2% off Costco, 5.2% off Target and 4.7% off Walmart in a day, about $29 billion, and forced grocers into a multi-year margin-destroying investment in delivery and price. Regulation and accounting: the FDA's July 28 2017 proposal to cut nicotine to non-addictive levels cut Altria 17% in a day, and on February 22 2019 Kraft Heinz disclosed a $15.4 billion brand writedown, a dividend cut and an SEC subpoena and fell 28% to a record low, discrediting the 3G model. By 2019 packaged food was being valued as a collection of melting ice cubes, while Walmart and Costco were pulling away as the sub-industry's only growth stories.\n\n## 2020-2022: hoarding, inflation and the retail margin shock\n\nCOVID briefly made staples momentum stocks. Panic buying lifted the packaged-food index 4.7% on March 2 2020, Clorox rose 7.9% in a day (most since 2011) and about 20% in a month, and Kroger was up 15% year-to-date on March 18 while the S&P 500 was down 26%. Leadership passed to reopening names in November 2020, and the 2021-2022 inflation then tested pricing power: the food and household companies pushed through double-digit price increases and staples outperformed sharply in the 2022 bear market, but the retailers could not keep up. Walmart fell 11% on May 17 2022 and Target 25% the next day, its worst day since 1987, as inflated inventory and fuel costs halved Target's operating margin to 5.3%. The consumer's rotation from goods to food and fuel favoured Walmart's grocery mix, which is why Walmart, not Target, emerged as the decade's winner.\n\n## 2023-2024: the GLP-1 scare, China beauty and the low-income consumer\n\nOctober 4 2023 was the sub-industry's most important day in a decade: Walmart's US CEO said GLP-1 users were already buying less, the staples gauge fell 2.7% on the Friday for its worst week since September 2022, and over the following year the five largest pure-play packaged-food stocks fell about 18% while the S&P 500 rose 24%. The fear faded in 2024 but the de-rating stuck. In household and personal products the casualty was Estee Lauder, which fell 18.9% on November 1 2023 on the failed China travel-retail recovery and lost more than $100 billion of value from its January 2022 peak. In staples retail the divide widened: Dollar General fell 32% on August 29 2024 (its worst day since IPO) as its low-income customer ran out of money and Walmart took share, and Walgreens, down 55% in the first half of 2024 and ejected from the Dow for Amazon, ended as a $10 billion Sycamore take-private in 2025.\n\n## 2025: MAHA, tariffs, restructurings and the Tylenol shock\n\nPolicy moved into the aisle. HHS announced on April 22 2025 a phase-out of six synthetic dyes by end-2026; the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (July 4) cut SNAP by $186 billion over ten years and widened work requirements, a slow headwind for dollar stores, discount grocers and the packaged-food volumes they carry; and the November 6 GLP-1 pricing deal ($245 a month for Medicare and Medicaid, Medicare obesity coverage, sub-$350 TrumpRx prices) revived the 2023 volume fears. The food and beverage companies responded with restructuring: Keurig Dr Pepper fell 11% on August 25 on its $18 billion JDE Peet's purchase and planned split, and Kraft Heinz announced on September 2 that it would undo its 2015 merger with the shares down 60% since. The year's strangest event was in household products: Trump's September 22 2025 claim linking Tylenol to autism sent Kenvue sharply lower, and on November 3 Kimberly-Clark agreed to buy it for $48.7 billion at a 46% premium, with Kenvue up 18% and Kimberly-Clark down 12.5% on the day. The deal closed on November 2 2026.\n\n## 2026: the defensive rotation\n\nStaples are having their best year since XLP launched in 1998. The fund was up about 13% by late February, the strongest start in its history, and reached a record high on November 4 2026, up roughly 17% year-to-date while consumer discretionary was flat and the S&P 500 lagged, as money rotated into Walmart, Costco, Coca-Cola and P&G on consumer-stress, tariff and AI-capex worries. The caution is that by February the sector already traded at its most expensive relative valuation in about 25 years on flat 2025 earnings, so the move is a positioning and rates story, not an earnings story, and the 2009, 2016 and 2020 precedents say it reverses when risk appetite returns.\n\n## What the sub-industries trade on today\n\n**Food, beverage and tobacco (PBJ).** The group trades on GLP-1 volume risk (re-opened by the November 2025 price cuts and TrumpRx), MAHA regulation (dye phase-out by end-2026, SNAP restrictions, ultra-processed-food labelling), private-label share gains and retailer pressure, and self-help restructurings (Kraft Heinz split, KDP's coffee separation). Tobacco trades on nicotine-pouch growth versus menthol and nicotine-cap risk, and the whole group on its dividend yield relative to Treasuries, which is why it is leading in 2026's defensive rotation.\n\n**Household and personal products.** Pricing-versus-volume elasticity after three years of price increases is the fundamental question; input costs (pulp, resin) and the dollar are the margin swing factors. Kimberly-Clark's integration of Kenvue and the Tylenol litigation overhang are the group's idiosyncratic stories; Estee Lauder and the beauty names remain hostage to China and travel retail; and the sub-industry carries the largest \"bond proxy\" sensitivity to the 10-year yield.\n\n**Staples retail and distribution.** The group has bifurcated. Walmart and Costco trade on share gains from trade-down, membership-fee economics, grocery inflation and their ability to absorb tariffs on general merchandise, and they are the main reason XLP is at records. Dollar stores, drugstores and Kroger-type grocers trade on SNAP cuts, the stretched low-income consumer and reimbursement pressure; Target sits in between as the discretionary-heavy laggard.\n","context_sectors":"# Context sectors: Financials, Utilities, Materials and Communication Services (Media)\n\n*Research note for the Capital Rotation Tracker (sector ids `financials`, `utilities`, `materials`, `communication_media`). Tracked at sector level with lighter sub-industry depth; they are where capital goes when it leaves technology, and vice versa. Prepared 7 November 2026.*\n\n## Financials\n\nFinancials trade on the yield curve, credit and regulation, and they were the epicentre of the one regime technology was not. The credit crunch began with the Fed's 50 bp cut on 18 September 2007; by February 2008 analysts expected financial profits to fall 23%; Lehman filed on 15 September 2008 and AIG fell 61% that day. The sector lost 82.6% from the October 2007 peak to 9 March 2009 and about 57% in calendar 2008. The May 2009 stress tests (10 of 19 banks needing $74.6 billion) ended the panic, and Dodd-Frank (21 July 2010) set the frame for a decade: Volcker Rule, Durbin interchange caps, the CFPB and stress testing, which capped returns and pushed activity toward non-banks and payment networks.\n\nThe 2010s were rate-driven rotations: worst sector in 2011 (-17.1%) on the US downgrade and Eurozone crisis, best in 2012 (+28.8%) on QE3 and housing. The 2016 election produced the best bank month on record (KBE +18.7% in November 2016) and the May 2018 rollback of enhanced supervision to $250 billion delivered part of that promise. COVID brought dividend caps and a buyback ban (25 June 2020) and a -1.7% year; 2021's reflation (+35.0%) reversed it. Silicon Valley Bank's failure on 10 March 2023 (KRE -14.4% in three sessions, First Republic -62% in a day) was the first deposit run of the smartphone era and ended with the BTFP and JPMorgan's purchase of First Republic.\n\nThree structural changes followed. GICS moved Visa, Mastercard, PayPal and Fiserv into Financials on 17 March 2023 (+2.7 points of index weight). The 2025 deregulation cycle (eSLR proposal 25 June 2025, finalised 25 November; Basel III endgame re-proposed with reductions on 19 March 2026; Capital One allowed to buy Discover) and the GENIUS Act of 18 July 2025 reopened bank M&A and made stablecoins a bank product. And credit stress returned outside the banks: First Brands' September 2025 bankruptcy exposed private-credit and trade-finance losses, and in October-November 2026 CoreWeave's bonds went distressed and hyperscaler CDS hit their widest since 2021. The Fed's 16 September 2026 hike helps margins, but XLF is up only 6.3% year-to-date against 40.6% for energy.\n\n## Utilities\n\nUtilities are the market's bond proxy; their relative performance mirrors the 10-year yield. They fell 45.9% in the GFC bear (less than the index) and led whenever yields collapsed: best sector of 2011 (+19.9%), +29.0% in 2014, +16.3% in 2016. The May 2013 taper tantrum was the defining risk event: -3.4% in the first week against -1.07% for the S&P 500 and +13.2% for the year versus +32.4%. PG&E's Camp Fire collapse (-52% on 14 January 2019, Chapter 11 on 29 January) added wildfire liability as a structural discount for Western utilities.\n\nThe story was rewritten twice after 2022. Utilities returned +1.6% in 2022 while the index fell 18.1%, then were the worst sector of 2023 (-7.1%) as the Fed's final hike pushed long yields to cycle highs. Then the AI-power narrative arrived: Talen's March 2024 campus sale to Amazon, Constellation's Three Mile Island restart for Microsoft (20 September 2024, +14% intraday) and Goldman's forecast that data centers would be the largest driver of US power demand through 2030 made utilities the top sector for much of 2024 (+23.4% for the year), led by Vistra (+108% year-to-date by September) and Constellation (+60%), with PJM capacity costs rising to more than $16 billion from $2.2 billion.\n\n2026 turned the trade political. New York suspended large data-center permits for a year on 14 July 2026, Chicago, Pennsylvania and New Jersey followed, at least 47 large projects were delayed or denied, and Gallup found 71% of Americans opposed to local data centers. After the 3 November Democratic sweep, Goldman estimated moratoria could affect 45% of planned capacity. With the 10-year up about a point from its February low and the Fed hiking, XLU is up only 3.5% year-to-date through 5 November 2026.\n\n## Materials\n\nMaterials trade on China, the dollar and the commodity cycle, with gold miners a separate real-yield and fear trade. The 2008 crash was total: oil from $147 on 11 July 2008 to $30 by December, the sector -59.6% peak-to-trough. China's stimulus produced the supercycle top of early 2011 (copper above $10,000 a tonne), after which the sector returned -9.6% in 2011 and declined into the 2015 bust (China devaluation, oil below $30, the Glencore debt scare; materials -8.4%). The 2016 reflation (+16.7%) and 2021 reopening (+27.3%) were the two cyclical rallies; 2018 (-14.7%) showed tariffs help steelmakers but hurt the sector when they slow global growth; 2024 (0.0%) was a dead year as China's property slump offset copper.\n\nPolicy has dominated since 2025: steel and aluminum tariffs doubled to 50% on 4 June 2025, a 50% copper tariff (9 July 2025) sent Comex copper to a record $5.65 a pound, and the April 2026 proclamation restructured the metals tariffs on a full-value basis. The state took direct equity in MP Materials on 10 July 2025 (shares +50%), the template later used for Intel. Gold was the standout: past $4,000 in October 2025, near $4,350 at year-end (+60%, best year since at least 1988), GDX up roughly 115-134%; the Iran war pushed gold to about $5,400 in March 2026 before a 21% Q2 drawdown in miners as yields rose. Lithium went the other way: Albemarle lost $511 million in 2025 and idled Kemerton in February 2026. XLB is up 16.9% year-to-date in 2026, behind energy but far ahead of technology.\n\n## Communication Services (Media and Entertainment)\n\nThe sector did not exist until 28 September 2018, when GICS folded Alphabet, Facebook, Netflix and Comcast in with the carriers and media groups. Before that, media was a story of capital leaving legacy distribution: Google's $1.65 billion YouTube purchase (October 2006) and Comcast's $30 billion NBC Universal deal (December 2009) bracketed the shift of advertising toward platforms, pay-TV households peaked in 2011 and cord-cutters became the majority in 2022. Disney+ (12 November 2019, 10 million day-one sign-ups) briefly re-rated legacy media as growth, until Netflix's first subscriber loss (19 April 2022, -35% in a day) ended the \"growth at any cost\" era and the sector finished 2022 at -39.9%, the worst in the index.\n\nThen the best and the worst years in its short history. Meta's \"year of efficiency\" (+150% in 2023), Netflix's ad tier and Alphabet's AI narrative drove returns of 55.8% in 2023, 40.2% in 2024 and 33.6% in 2025, the top sector in both 2024 and 2025. Legacy media consolidated: Skydance closed its Paramount merger on 7 August 2025, WBD jumped 33% on Paramount's approach in September 2025, EA agreed a record $55 billion buyout on 29 September 2025, and Paramount outbid Netflix for WBD at $110 billion enterprise value on 27 February 2026 (closed 6 October 2026). In 2026 the growth composition became a liability: Alphabet fell 7% on 23 July on a $205 billion capex guide and 13% ($490 billion) on 29 October after a second quarter of negative free cash flow, and XLC is the worst sector of 2026 at -10.6% year-to-date.\n\n## What the sectors trade on today\n\n**Financials.** The curve after a Fed hiking again (3.75-4% since 16 September 2026), the pace of capital relief (eSLR final, Basel re-proposal), M&A approval, and credit: private-credit exposures, AI-infrastructure lending and the widest hyperscaler CDS since 2021. Banks gain from higher short rates and deregulation; capital markets and insurers carry the AI credit cycle and long yields at multi-year highs. Stablecoin economics (GENIUS Act, Circle, Coinbase) are the growth sub-plot.\n\n**Utilities.** The 10-year yield first, then the political durability of data-center load growth: state moratoria, PJM capacity prices and affordability politics under a Democratic Congress, wildfire liability and allowed ROEs. Regulated names trade as bond proxies again; independent power producers trade with the AI complex and sold off with it in October-November 2026.\n\n**Materials.** Gold miners trade on real yields, central-bank buying and geopolitics, with record free cash flow at $4,400 gold and sensitivity to every Fed signal. Metals and mining trade on the Section 232 regime, copper's grid and data-center demand, and China. Chemicals trade on natural-gas feedstock after the Hormuz shock, destocking and industrial production, with 50% metals tariffs raising downstream costs.\n\n**Communication Services.** Capex intensity and free cash flow at Alphabet and Meta, search disruption and antitrust appeals, the ad cycle, streaming profitability and sports-rights inflation, and the integration of Paramount-WBD under heavy debt. The sector is the first casualty of AI-capex doubt because its two largest members are also two of the largest spenders.\n","energy":"# Energy: where the capital went, 1999-2026\n\n*Total returns; sources in `events_group1.json`.*\n\n## Preamble: 1999-2005\n\nEnergy was a 6% weight and a value backwater at the Nasdaq peak, and the first rotation of the century was into it: XLE returned 24% in 2000 while the S&P 500 lost 10%. The 2001-02 recession took oil back under $20, but from the March 2003 low the China supercycle made energy the market's leadership sector for five years. XLE beat the index every year from 2003 to 2007 (40% in 2005, 37% in 2007), the sector rose roughly 300% trough to peak, and its weight climbed toward 16% by mid-2008. Refining had its own golden age inside the boom: no new US refinery since 1976 plus Katrina and Rita in 2005 gave Valero 98% in 2004 and 128% in 2005.\n\n## 2008-2010: $147 to $32 and the shale rebound\n\nWTI peaked at $147 on July 11, 2008 and fell to about $32 by December. Leverage showed in the returns: XLE -39% in 2008, E&Ps (XOP) -43%, services (OIH) -60%, Valero -69%, while net-cash Exxon fell 13%. The 2008 solar bubble burst in parallel (TAN lost three-quarters of its value in five months). The rebound was led by beta (OIH +64% in 2009, XOP +40% and +29% in 2009-10). Shale also created the midstream yield trade: zero rates and new pipelines out of the Bakken and Marcellus pulled retail money into MLPs and made Cheniere, the first US LNG exporter, a three-time triple-digit stock.\n\n## 2011-2013: solar's second bust and the refiners' spread\n\nChinese capacity collapsed module prices just as Europe cut subsidies, and Solyndra's bankruptcy on August 31, 2011 made solar politically toxic: TAN -63% in 2011, First Solar -74%. The sector rebounded 128% in 2013 on stable polysilicon and the yieldco model. For fossil energy the story was the Cushing glut: shale crude trapped mid-continent put WTI at a $15-22 discount to Brent in 2012, so refiners bought cheap and sold at world prices (Marathon Petroleum +89%, Valero +66% in 2012, Valero +65% in 2013).\n\n## 2014-2016: OPEC's price war and the MLP collapse\n\nWith US shale adding a million barrels a day a year, OPEC refused to cut on November 27, 2014 and crude fell 10% that day; XOP was already 40% off its June high, and WTI bottomed near $26 in February 2016. XOP lost 29% and 36% in 2014-15 and XLE 21.5% in a flat 2015, with dozens of bankruptcies. The MLP model broke in the same crash: the Alerian index fell 47% and Kinder Morgan cut its dividend 75% on December 8, 2015. Refiners were again the only winners (Valero +47% in 2015) and SunEdison's April 2016 bankruptcy put solar back in a bust (TAN -43%). The turn came with OPEC's first cut since 2008 on November 30, 2016: XLE rose 5% in a day and finished 2016 up 28%, XOP 38%, KMI 43%.\n\n## 2017-2019: over-production and the shrinking weight\n\nThree years of volume growth over returns made energy the worst sector of the late-cycle bull market. XOP lost money in 2017, 2018 and 2019, OIH lost 45% in 2018 alone, and the Q4 2018 crash (Brent -37% from its October peak, Exxon -14% in December) came as the sector's weight fell toward 4%. Generalists exited, which is why the 2020-21 reversal was so violent.\n\n## 2020-2021: negative oil, clean-energy mania, discipline rebound\n\nThe OPEC+ breakdown on March 6, 2020 and lockdowns took the sector down about 50% in Q1; on April 20 the May WTI contract settled at -$37.63, and on August 24 Exxon left the Dow with energy at 2.4% of the S&P 500. XLE lost 33% for the year. At the same moment zero rates, ESG inflows and the Biden platform produced the largest clean-energy bubble on record (ICLN +142%, TAN +234%, Enphase +572% in 2020), peaking in February 2021. The two halves then swapped: rising yields took ICLN down 24% in 2021 while reopening demand and the new shale model (flat volumes, buybacks, variable dividends) gave fossil energy its best year since 2005 (XLE +53%, XOP +67%, AMLP +39%, Exxon +58%).\n\n## 2022-2025: Ukraine, the IRA, consolidation, the AI power trade\n\nRussia's invasion made energy the only positive sector of 2022 (XLE +64% against -20%, the widest outperformance on record): Exxon +87%, SLB +81% as international and offshore work resumed, refiners at record margins (Marathon earned $28.17 a share) and LNG exporters re-rated by Europe's gas scramble. The Inflation Reduction Act (August 16, 2022) gave clean energy a brief rally and a decade of credits, but 5% yields and inventory gluts produced a third solar bust: TAN -27% in 2023 and -38% in 2024, Enphase -50% both years, and a further plunge when Trump won in November 2024.\n\nWith oil flat around $75, 2023-24 belonged to consolidation and gas. Exxon-Pioneer (~$60bn) and Chevron-Hess ($53bn) in October 2023 started a wave that shrank the public E&P universe; XLE was flat in 2023 and up 6% in 2024. Pipelines became an AI derivative (Kinder Morgan +64% in 2024) on data-center and LNG demand, and the nuclear/IPP trade was born: the July 2024 PJM auction clearing at $270/MW-day (from $29) and Constellation's Microsoft deal to restart Three Mile Island sent Vistra up 262% and Constellation 93% in 2024. DeepSeek knocked 20-30% off the IPPs on January 27, 2025 and Trump's May 23 nuclear orders sent Oklo up 130% in a month.\n\n2025 was a down year for the commodity. OPEC+ tripled its planned hike the day after the Liberation Day tariffs, WTI hit $61.99 and oil spent the year in the $60s (XLE +8%, XOP -2% against +18% for the index); oilfield services hit multiyear lows while refiners decoupled on capacity closures (Valero +37%). Clean energy whipsawed on the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (Sunrun -39% and -40% on the House and Senate drafts, +8% when leasing survived); First Solar ended up 48% on tariffs and AI power demand, Enphase down 53%.\n\n## 2026: the Hormuz war\n\nIsrael struck Iran on February 28, 2026 and the US launched Operation Epic Fury. Drones hit Ras Tanura and Qatar's Ras Laffan LNG plant, insurers cancelled Hormuz war-risk cover and transits collapsed from 85 a day to near zero. Brent went from the $60s to $84 on March 3, $109 on March 9 and $115 in late October before easing toward $98-100 in November; the Fed hiked in September on the resulting inflation. Energy is 2026's best sector: XLE +41% year to date against 8% for the S&P 500, Cheniere +45% on the LNG premium, and refiners the biggest winners as diesel hit a record $5.90 a gallon (Valero +119%). The IPPs went the other way: after three record PJM auctions turned power prices into an affordability issue, Constellation and Vistra are down about 30%. The Democratic midterm sweep lifted solar 8-9% on November 4 from a low base (First Solar -23%, Sunrun -49% year to date).\n\n## What the sub-industries trade on today\n\n**Integrated oil and gas.** Brent in the $95-115 range set by the Hormuz closure, and whether a settlement reopens the strait and takes $30-40 out of the price. Exxon and Chevron trade on free cash flow at $100 oil, buyback capacity and the Permian/Guyana growth they bought in 2023; they are the sector's defensive core.\n\n**Exploration and production.** Pure commodity beta with capital discipline: XOP moves 1.5-2x oil. Gas-weighted names (EQT, Expand, Antero) trade on LNG feedgas and data-center load; oil names on OPEC+ policy once the war premium fades. Consolidation has left fewer, larger, lower-beta names.\n\n**Refining and marketing.** Crack spreads and product shortages. With Gulf refineries damaged and diesel at records, US refiners capture the global product deficit; the risk is a ceasefire that normalizes margins as fast as 2024 did (CRAK -15%). Capacity closures and RIN costs are the structural variables.\n\n**Midstream and pipelines.** Fee-based yield that trades on gas volume growth from LNG and data centers, permitting reform and the 10-year yield. It lagged the 2026 upstream rally because it has little oil exposure, but LNG exporters are direct beneficiaries of Qatar's outage.\n\n**Oilfield equipment and services.** International and offshore capex is strong; North American pressure pumping still shrinks with the rig count and E&P discipline. OIH is up 22% in 2026 after two down years; the group trades on whether $100 oil finally lifts US drilling budgets.\n\n**New energy, nuclear and independent power.** Three trades. Solar trades on policy (OBBBA phase-outs, December panel tariffs, a Democratic Congress against a presidential veto) and rates. Nuclear and uranium trade on SMR approvals, executive orders and hyperscaler PPAs. The IPPs trade on PJM capacity prices and the political backlash against them, which is why Constellation and Vistra fell 30% in a year of record power demand.\n","healthcare":"# Health Care: where the capital went, 1999-2026\n\n*Companion data: `research/events_group2.json` (ids `hc-`). Series: XLV; IHE, IBB, XBI, IHI, IHF; baskets for tools, managed care and health-care technology.*\n\n## Preamble, 1999-2005: three regimes in six years\n\nHealth care entered the century as a defensive sector with a speculative appendage. Genomics stocks were bid up as the biological internet, and broke first: on March 14 2000 a Clinton-Blair statement on open genome data was misread as the end of gene patents and the Nasdaq Biotech Index fell 12.5% in a day. The rest of the sector held value through the 2000-2002 bear market. Two shocks then set the pattern for everything after: the Medicare Modernization Act (December 2003) created Part D and modern Medicare Advantage, and managed care re-rated for two years on its new subsidised market (UnitedHealth took 27% of Part D enrollment at launch); and Merck's Vioxx withdrawal (September 30 2004, stock -27% in a day) reminded investors that pharma cash flows carry binary safety risk. By 2006 pharma was a value trade, managed care a growth trade and biotech a venture trade.\n\n## 2006-2012: the ACA years, hospitals versus insurers\n\nThe ACA fight created the rule that **hospitals and insurers trade against each other on coverage policy**. Obama's 2008 win took Cigna down 40% and WellPoint 15% within a month. When the House passed the law on March 21 2010, Tenet rose 9% and HMA 11% while UnitedHealth fell 3%: more insured patients for providers, margin caps and fees for commercial insurers. The June 28 2012 Supreme Court decision repeated the move (HCA +10.75%, big insurers -3 to -4%) and locked in a 2.3% device excise tax that lasted until its 2019 repeal. Hospitals and Medicaid insurers are long coverage, commercial insurers are short regulatory margin pressure, and the legs swap leadership on every election and ruling.\n\n## 2012-2016: the biotech supercycle and its unwind\n\nHepatitis C cures, immuno-oncology, the 2012 JOBS Act and zero rates produced the sector's greatest bull market: IBB rose about 214% from January 2012 to mid-2015 and capital flowed out of pharma and devices into anything with a Phase 2 asset. The top was political. Hillary Clinton's September 21 2015 \"price gouging\" tweet cut IBB 4.6% in a day and 13% for the week, and drug-pricing politics, Valeant's collapse and the August 2015 China scare took XBI down 49% to February 2016, worse than 2008. SMID biotech is a liquidity and risk-appetite instrument first and a science instrument second.\n\n## 2016-2019: election beta\n\nTrump's 2016 win was read as ACA repeal: Tenet fell 25% and HCA 11% on November 9 2016 while pharma rallied. Repeal failed (the AHCA was pulled on March 24 2017; the Senate \"skinny repeal\" died on July 28) and heavily shorted hospitals squeezed back. The January 30 2018 Amazon-Berkshire-JPMorgan venture took $69 billion out of PBMs, pharmacies and insurers in a day. In April 2019, with Medicare for All gaining sponsors, UnitedHealth's CEO attacked it on his own earnings call and S&P 500 health care lost $150 billion in four days; \"political beta\" ruled managed care until the 2020 primaries.\n\n## 2020-2021: pandemic winners\n\nCOVID split the sector. CMS told hospitals to postpone elective surgery on March 18 2020, so device makers and hospitals lost their revenue base while diagnostics and bioprocessing, telehealth (Teladoc $83 to $294) and mRNA vaccines (Moderna: $18.5 billion of 2021 revenue) absorbed the inflows. Pfizer's November 9 2020 efficacy news began the reopening rotation, and zero rates plus a record IPO/SPAC calendar pushed XBI to an all-time high on February 8 2021. Tools spent their windfalls on CROs (Thermo-PPD, $17.4 billion, April 2021).\n\n## 2021-2023: the rate shock and the GLP-1 split\n\nRising real yields repriced long-duration health care first and hardest. XBI fell 63.9% from February 2021 to May 2022, the deepest drawdown in its history; Teladoc wrote down $13.4 billion of Livongo goodwill and fell 88% from its peak; tools took the second-order hit as biotech customers destocked (Danaher cut guidance in April 2023, Thermo in October). The Inflation Reduction Act (August 16 2022) added Medicare price negotiation. Meanwhile capital concentrated into GLP-1s: Novo became Europe's most valuable company in 2023 and Lilly's Zepbound was approved on November 8 2023. The same drugs created a \"scare trade\" on October 4 2023 when Walmart said GLP-1 users were buying less, hitting Dexcom (-30% year-to-date) and Insulet (-54%) and CPAP suppliers. And on June 14 2023 UnitedHealth flagged surging senior outpatient surgery, the start of the Medicare Advantage cost problem: insurers fell, Stryker and HCA rose.\n\n## 2024-2025: managed care breaks, policy risk everywhere\n\n2024 brought the Change Healthcare ransomware attack (February 21; $872 million of Q1 costs), a flat Medicare Advantage rate notice (April 2; insurers -6 to -12%), the first negotiated Medicare prices (August 15; 22% aggregate cut) and RFK Jr.'s HHS nomination (November 14; Moderna -5.6%). 2025 was worse. UnitedHealth cut guidance and fell 23% on April 17, lost its CEO on May 13, fell 13% more on May 15 on the DOJ criminal probe report, and ended the year down 35%; Centene pulled guidance on July 1 and fell 40%. The One Big Beautiful Bill Act (July 4) cut Medicaid by about $1 trillion over ten years and the enhanced ACA subsidies drifted toward expiry through a 39-day shutdown. Pharma faced the MFN order (May 12), the 100% tariff threat (September 25) and company-by-company deals, culminating in the November 6 GLP-1 agreement ($245 a month for government programs, Medicare obesity coverage) that also killed compounded-GLP-1 telehealth (Hims had lost 35% in a day when Novo cut it off on June 23). Novo cut guidance on July 29; Lilly hit $1 trillion on November 21. NIH freezes and more than 5,800 grant terminations weighed on tools.\n\n## 2026: biotech leads, devices lag, insurers whipsaw\n\nThree things define 2026. Medicare Advantage: the January 26 advance notice proposed a 0.09% rate change and UnitedHealth and Humana lost a fifth of their value in a day; the April 6 final notice delivered 2.48% and they recovered 10-11% in a session. Tariffs: the April 2 Section 232 proclamation imposed 100% duties on patented drugs with 15% carve-outs for the EU, Japan, Korea and Switzerland and exemptions for companies that signed deals; because the Supreme Court's February 20 ruling voided IEEPA tariffs but not Section 232, this regime binds. Rotation: XBI is up about 25% year-to-date and IBB 27% as of November 3, while IHI is the only major health-care ETF in the red.\n\n## What the sub-industries trade on today\n\n**Pharmaceuticals (IHE).** Trades on policy deal status more than pipelines: companies with MFN/TrumpRx and onshoring agreements (Lilly, Pfizer, J&J) are exempt from the 100% Section 232 tariff that bites from July-September 2026; European and Japanese exporters are not. GLP-1 is the only growth engine and the 2026-2030 patent cliff keeps M&A appetite high.\n\n**Biotech, large cap (IBB).** Up 27% in 2026 on pipeline readouts, pharma M&A, the IRA negotiation calendar and FDA predictability. Vaccine and mRNA names stay de-rated by HHS policy; Moderna's revenue fell from $18.5 billion (2021) to $1.9 billion (2025).\n\n**Biotech, SMID cap (XBI).** The sector's risk-appetite gauge. The 2026 rally (+25% YTD) rests on lower rates, reopened IPO windows and takeouts, but XBI has not regained its February 2021 high and cooled 1.5% in the past month. It lives and dies on real yields and FTC/FDA posture.\n\n**Life sciences tools and services.** Recovering from the 2023 destocking; trades on biotech funding (improving with XBI), NIH budgets (about 2,600 grants worth $1.4 billion still suspended in January 2026), China demand and tariffs on instruments.\n\n**Medical devices and equipment (IHI).** The 2026 laggard: sold on rotation into biotech, tariff exposure on imported components and recurring GLP-1 volume fears whenever GLP-1 prices fall. Procedure volumes remain solid, so the de-rating is positioning, not earnings.\n\n**Managed care.** Trades on the CMS calendar: the January advance and April final Medicare Advantage notices (2026: -20% then +10%), October Star ratings, Medicaid rates after OBBBA, exchange morbidity after the subsidy cliff, and the DOJ's UnitedHealth investigations. Cost trend is the fundamental; policy is the volatility.\n\n**Providers, facilities and distribution (IHF).** Hospitals trade on coverage: the $1 trillion Medicaid cut and subsidy expiry are a 2027-2034 volume and bad-debt headwind, partly offset by a $50 billion rural fund, while near-term volumes are favourable. Distributors ride GLP-1 volumes; PBM reform and 340B are the overhangs.\n\n**Health care technology.** The pandemic cohort never recovered; the group trades on real yields, GLP-1 telehealth economics now that the November 2025 price deal has ended compounding, provider IT budgets and clinical AI adoption.\n","industrials":"# Industrials: where the capital went, 1999-2026\n\n*Total returns; sources in `events_group1.json`.*\n\n## Preamble: 1999-2005\n\nIndustrials entered the century as the \"old economy\" and suffered with the tech bust anyway: XLI lost 10% in 2001 and 25% in 2002 as business investment collapsed, and 9/11 broke the airlines (UAL -42% and US Airways -52% on the first trading day; United, US Airways, Delta and Northwest all filed Chapter 11 by 2005). The recovery came from China. From the March 2003 low, Chinese infrastructure and mining demand made heavy machinery the leadership group of the bull market: Caterpillar returned 86% in 2003, Deere 99% in 2007, and XLI beat the S&P 500 in 2003-04.\n\n## 2007-2010: crash and early-cycle rebound\n\nIndustrials fell further than the market in the GFC. XLI's drawdown was 62% (October 2007 to March 2009) against 55% for SPY, and the ETF lost 39% in 2008: United Rentals -50%, Deere -58%, Boeing -50%. The defensive wing held: Waste Management was positive in 2008 and Lockheed fell only 19%, because garbage is contracted and the defense budget was still growing with two wars.\n\nThe rebound was equally lopsided. Once credit reopened, capital rotated back into the names it had abandoned: XLI returned 22% in 2009 and 28% in 2010, United Rentals rose 132% in 2010, Deere 45% and 56%. A second China mining boom took Caterpillar up 68% in 2010; Caterpillar's own 2015 restructuring deck dates that cycle's peak to 2012, after which sales fell 31%.\n\n## 2011-2014: sequestration, the Boeing supercycle, housing\n\nThe August 2011 Budget Control Act created the threat of automatic defense cuts; primes traded at valuations \"not seen since the end of the Cold War\" and federal services stocks lagged for two years. The 2013 lesson is worth remembering: the sequester took effect on March 1, 2013 and defense had its best year in decades (ITA +56%, Lockheed +68%) because the worst case was priced and the companies bought back stock. Commercial aerospace ran its own supercycle: Boeing lifted 737 output to 38 a month on a $415bn backlog and returned 85% in 2013. Building products joined on the housing recovery and QE3's record-low mortgage rates (PKB +41% in 2013).\n\n## 2015-2016: the industrial recession\n\nThe oil crash, a 20% dollar rally and China's slowdown produced a manufacturing recession inside an expansion. Fastenal's CEO said in October 2015 that 44 of its top 100 customers were shrinking, railroads entered a bear market on coal and crude carloads, and Dow Transports lagged the S&P 500 by 1,800bp. Caterpillar, United Rentals and Old Dominion each lost 23-29% in 2015; the trough came in February 2016.\n\n## 2016-2019: reflation, GE, tariffs, the MAX\n\nTrump's election triggered the sharpest rotation into industrials since 2009: the group rose 4.4% in three sessions and 9% in a month on infrastructure and tax-cut hopes, Caterpillar returned 42% in 2016 and 75% in 2017, and XLI gained 24% in 2017. The February 2018 Bipartisan Budget Act lifted defense to $700bn and $716bn, 15% above the caps.\n\nTwo shocks ended the decade. GE halved its dividend in November 2017 as its gas-turbine business collapsed, fell 57% in two years and left the Dow in June 2018. The 2018 tariffs made Boeing and Caterpillar the trade-war proxies: XLI lost 13% in 2018 against 5% for the index, United Rentals fell 40% and Caterpillar's record profits could not stop a 12% slide. The 737 MAX grounding in March 2019 (Boeing -11% in two days, 20 months on the ground) removed the sector's growth engine just before COVID.\n\n## 2020-2021: pandemic, vaccine, goods boom\n\nCOVID split the sector. JETS fell 59% between February 21 and March 23, 2020 (SPY -33%), United lost 49% in March, Berkshire sold its airlines and Boeing lost two-thirds of its value peak to trough. Defense sat out the entire 2020-21 bull market (ITA -14% then +9% against +18% and +29% for the S&P 500) as budgets flattened and ESG mandates excluded weapons makers, which set up the 2022 re-rating. The November 9, 2020 vaccine news began a reopening rotation that became 2021's goods boom: Old Dominion +85%, Robert Half +81%, United Rentals +43%, ADP +43%, XLI +21%. The IIJA, signed November 15, 2021, added a decade of funded demand; PAVE has returned more than 96% since.\n\n## 2022-2025: Ukraine, rates, freight recession, AI power\n\nRussia's invasion on February 24, 2022 ended defense's drought: Lockheed and Northrop each returned about 40% in a year the S&P 500 lost 18%. The rate shock (mortgages from 3% to 7%) de-rated building products (PKB -22%) while waste absorbed the defensive bid (WM -4.5%). Freight rolled over in mid-2022 and stayed depressed for three years; Old Dominion lost money in 2022, 2024 and 2025.\n\nThe dominant rotation began with Nvidia's May 2023 guidance: everything that powers and cools data centers re-rated. Vertiv returned 252% in 2023 and 137% in 2024, Eaton 56% and 40%, EMCOR 111% in 2024, and GE Vernova, spun out in April 2024, returned 99% in 2025. Comfort Systems' backlog doubled to $17.8bn; DeepSeek's January 27, 2025 scare (GE Vernova -18%, Vertiv -24% intraday) was fully recovered.\n\nPolicy drove 2025. Boeing's door-plug blowout, FAA cap and eight-week strike cost it 32% in 2024. Defense whipsawed on Hegseth's 8% cut memo (February) and Trump's $1 trillion budget (April), then re-rated globally on ReArm Europe (€800bn, March 4) and NATO's 5% target (June 25): ITA returned 49%. Liberation Day tariffs hit heavy equipment and transports hardest before the 90-day pause; XLI still finished 2025 up 19%. Professional services went the other way: Robert Half lost 59% and ADP 10% as investors priced AI agents replacing hiring and payroll seats, a selloff that climaxed on February 3, 2026 when Anthropic's Claude Cowork plug-ins triggered a $285bn one-day rout.\n\n## 2026: war, rates, midterms\n\nThe Iran war that began February 28, 2026 roughly doubled jet fuel; airlines cut capacity and 2026 EPS estimates were slashed (United from $13.33 to $9.46) before fare increases produced a June rebound. Defense rallied on Operation Epic Fury, a $200bn supplemental and a $1.5 trillion FY2027 request (Lockheed +21% year to date), then fell 7% the day after the November 3 Democratic sweep. Staffing staged the year's biggest squeeze (ManpowerGroup +120% from its March low by August). Boeing fell 10% on October 27 after pushing its 737 ramp out a year, and the Fed's September 16 hike plus a 5.1% ten-year hit building products. XLI is up about 6% against 8% for the S&P 500: a stock-picker's market rather than a sector rotation.\n\n## What the sub-industries trade on today\n\n**Defense.** The topline is a political variable: a $1.5 trillion request, a $901bn baseline, a House number near $1.15 trillion and a Democratic Congress. Stocks trade on reconciliation money for Golden Dome and munitions, European procurement under the 5% target, and Iran supplementals; the pure primes are more volatile than at any time since 2011.\n\n**Commercial aerospace.** Boeing's production stability and free cash flow: the 737 ramp to 47/month slipped into 2027, 777X certification is mid-2027, the 2026 delivery target was missed. GE Aerospace, TransDigm and HEICO trade on flying hours and aftermarket pricing, which stay strong because the fleet is old and new jets are late.\n\n**Transportation.** Airlines trade on jet fuel against premium-cabin demand; trucking and intermodal on whether the three-year freight recession ends; rails on volumes and tariff-driven import patterns. Cheap, but without a catalyst since 2021.\n\n**Machinery.** A split: data-center power generation and mining are strong, construction is rate-sensitive, and ag is emerging from a two-year farm-income downturn (Deere +44% year to date). Steel and component tariffs remain the margin variable.\n\n**Electrical equipment and electrification.** Hyperscaler capex guidance, grid spending and transformer lead times. Backlogs are at records (GE Vernova heading above $200bn), so the debate is multiple and political risk to data-center siting; capex scares produce 15-25% one-day drawdowns.\n\n**Industrial distribution and rental.** Short-cycle production and non-residential construction (mega-projects, data centers); United Rentals is up 30% in 2026 on reshoring work, Fastenal and Grainger on pricing pass-through and the rate-sensitive pipeline.\n\n**Building products and construction/engineering.** Two markets: EMCOR, Comfort Systems and Quanta trade on data-center and grid backlog; HVAC and residential names trade on 7%-plus mortgages and housing turnover. IIJA outlays still flow, with less than half the money awarded by early 2025.\n\n**Professional and commercial services.** The AI-substitution debate is the whole story; the 2026 rebound says the market over-shot, but every model release reprices the group. Float income on client funds gives ADP and Paychex a positive rate sensitivity.\n\n**Waste and environmental services.** A low-volatility compounder that trades as a defensive factor, outperforming in drawdowns (2008, 2022) and lagging in risk-on rallies (2023, mid-2025). Pricing above CPI, landfill scarcity and renewable natural gas are the fundamentals; the premium multiple is the risk.\n","real_estate":"# Real Estate: where the capital went, 1999-2026\n\n*FTSE Nareit total returns; sources in `events_group1.json`.*\n\n## Preamble: 1999-2005\n\nREITs were the anti-Nasdaq. Shunned in 1998-99, they absorbed the rotation out of growth when the bubble burst: +26% in 2000, +14% in 2001, +4% in 2002 against three down years for the S&P 500, with mortgage REITs +77% in 2001 as the Fed cut and the curve steepened. The 2003-06 bull market compounded prices 99% in three years on cheap debt, CMBS growth and private-equity takeovers, ending with Blackstone's $39bn Equity Office buyout in February 2007; 2006 alone returned 35%, with office, health care, self storage and apartments all above 40%. The warning was 2005, when seventeen Fed hikes flattened the curve and mortgage REITs lost 23% (Annaly -41%) while equity REITs rose 12%: the mortgage sub-sector trades on the yield curve, not on property.\n\n## 2007-2009: crash and recapitalization\n\nPrices peaked in February 2007. Subprime failures and the August 2007 money-market seizure closed the CMBS market; REITs lost 16% in 2007 and mortgage REITs 42%, then 38% in 2008 as Lehman raised solvency fears for every leveraged landlord. Dispersion was enormous: industrial REITs -67%, lodging -60%, regional malls -61% (General Growth filed the largest REIT bankruptcy on April 16, 2009), commercial mortgage REITs -75%. Self storage was the only positive sector (+5%). The bottom was made by equity: starting with Simon in March 2009, REITs raised $37.5bn in 91 secondary offerings, and the most distressed groups led the 28% rebound (lodging +67%, malls +63%, SL Green +102%).\n\n## 2010-2016: the QE yield trade and its tantrum\n\nWith the 10-year falling from 3.3% to 1.8% over 2010-12, REITs became the retail bond substitute: +28%, +8% and +20%. Apartments led (+47% in 2010) as homeownership fell, self storage returned 35% in 2011, and 2012 brought the first \"new economy\" REITs when American Tower and Equinix converted (Equinix +103%).\n\nBernanke's May 22, 2013 taper remarks exposed the duration risk: mortgage REITs fell 21% in six weeks and Annaly cut its dividend from $0.45 to $0.30, net lease, health care and apartments went negative, and REITs returned 3% in a year the index gained 32%. The 2014 reversal, when yields fell anyway, gave REITs 28% (apartments +40%, health care +33%). HCP's $1.3bn of impairments on skilled-nursing tenant HCR ManorCare (2015-16) pushed capital toward private-pay senior housing, and self storage's 41% 2015 triggered a development wave that left it flat for three years. The 10-year's record 1.36% in July 2016 and the GICS sector launch on September 1, 2016 marked the top of the bond-proxy trade; it reversed the week Trump was elected.\n\n## 2017-2019: retail apocalypse, logistics boom, Fed pivot\n\nE-commerce moved capital from one REIT sector to another. Store closures ran at 7,000 a year from 2017, shopping centers lost 11% and 15% in 2017-18 and malls fell three years running. Industrial REITs returned 31%, 21% and 49% in 2016-19, data centers 44% in 2019 and towers 42% on 5G; by 2019 those groups were about a third of REIT market cap. The Powell pivot gave all REITs 29% in 2019.\n\n## 2020-2021: COVID and the best year on record\n\nCOVID produced the widest dispersion in REIT history. Mortgage REITs lost 56% in Q1 2020 on repo margin calls, Host suspended its dividend, lodging finished -24%, malls -37%, office -18%, apartments -15%; data centers (+21%), industrial (+12%) and self storage (+13%) were the only positive sectors. The November 9, 2020 vaccine news began the best REIT year ever: +41% in 2021, with malls +92% (Simon +96%), shopping centers +65%, self storage +79%, apartments +64% on record rent growth and industrial +62% (Prologis +72%); office (+22%) and lodging (+18%) lagged.\n\n## 2022-2023: rate shock and the regional-bank office collapse\n\nThe 10-year rose 243bp in 2022 and REITs lost 25%, the worst year since 2008: office -38%, apartments -32%, self storage -27%, mortgage REITs -27%, with only net lease (-6.5%) holding up. Two narrative drawdowns compounded it: Amazon's April 2022 admission of excess warehouse space ended the logistics premium (Prologis -31%) and Jim Chanos's short of \"legacy\" data centers took Digital Realty down 41%.\n\nSilicon Valley Bank's failure on March 10, 2023 hit the banks that make 70% of CRE loans: SL Green fell 37% in a month, Vornado 27%, office returned 2% in a year all REITs returned 11%, and brokers' transaction revenue halved as global CRE investment fell 48%. Medical Properties Trust's Steward saga (-82% from peak) was health care's own credit event.\n\n## 2023-2025: AI landlords, senior housing, a muted market\n\nThe Chanos thesis reversed within a year: AI leasing (Digital Realty signed more than $520m in Q3 2024) made data centers the top sector in 2023 (+30%) and 2024 (+25%), while towers lagged three years on carrier capex cuts and Sprint churn (telecom -14% in 2024; Crown Castle sold its fiber unit and cut its dividend 32% in March 2025). Health care became the structural winner: the 80-plus wave and zero new senior-housing supply gave Welltower 14% same-store NOI growth and the sector 14%, 24% and 28.5% in 2023-25.\n\nThe rest was rate-driven and muted. The September 2024 cut produced the best quarter since 2009 (+16.8%, Realty Income from $50 to $65) and the post-election yield rebound took it back (-8% in Q4); REITs returned 5% in 2024 and 2% in 2025. Beneath the index: the Sunbelt supply wave turned apartment rents negative (-9% in 2025), office bounced 21.5% in 2024 on return-to-office and lost 14% in 2025, self storage fell 10% as housing turnover hit 30-year lows, industrial REITs hit five-year lows after the Liberation Day tariffs before finishing +17%, data centers were the worst sector of 2025 (-14%) and agency mortgage REITs the best (+26%). In services, the NAR settlement knocked 10-14% off Zillow and Compass in a day, Opendoor became a meme stock and Compass bought Anywhere for $1.6bn.\n\n## 2026: a good REIT year with a rate scare\n\nREITs returned 14.9% in the first half and are up about 13% year to date, beating a market whose AI rally lost steam. Leadership is fundamental: lodging +57% through October on record travel and no supply, data centers +28-33% on Digital Realty's record 340 MW quarter, health care +19% (Welltower the only sector above NAV), self storage +10% and malls +13-29% (Simon's best rent growth in a decade). Iran-war inflation then forced the Fed's first hike since 2023 on September 16; the 10-year hit 5.10% on September 23, every property type fell in September (net lease -10.6%, mortgage REITs -13.7%) and 30-year mortgages reached 7.19%. October's 4.7% rebound came with a Fed pause and an 18% jump in towers when SpaceX-cellular fears reversed. The laggards are office (-11% through September), residential (negative for the year) and mortgage REITs (-12%).\n\n## What the sub-industries trade on today\n\n**Industrial and logistics REITs.** Tariff and import headlines versus reshoring and data-center land demand; new supply has collapsed, so mark-to-market rent spreads drive earnings. Prologis is up only 3% in 2026 after +25% in 2025 and remains rate-sensitive.\n\n**Data center and tower REITs.** Two assets. Data centers trade on hyperscale leasing, power availability and the self-build threat at 25-30x FFO; towers on carrier capex, leverage and now satellite-network risk, still below 2021 highs.\n\n**Residential REITs.** Supply. Sunbelt deliveries are peaking and coastal landlords outperform; the sector is negative for 2026 and waits for 2027 rent growth as starts have collapsed. Rent-control politics and 7% mortgages are the secondary drivers.\n\n**Office and life-science REITs.** CMBS maturities, regional-bank lending, utilization data and conversions. The 2024 squeeze showed how fast the group moves on rates, but it is the worst sector of 2026 and trades on balance sheets and trophy leasing.\n\n**Retail REITs.** Zero new supply, record occupancy and re-leasing spreads from tenant failures; malls have gone from pariah to core holding. The risk is a consumer slowdown under $4-plus gasoline.\n\n**Net lease and specialty REITs.** The purest bond proxies: Realty Income and peers trade on the 10-year against acquisition cap rates, which is why they fell hardest in September 2026. Tenant credit is the idiosyncratic risk.\n\n**Health care REITs.** Senior-housing occupancy and NOI growth against the 80-plus wave, with minimal new supply; Welltower's all-in pivot is the template. Skilled-nursing and hospital landlords still trade on operator credit and Medicaid policy.\n\n**Self-storage REITs.** Housing turnover and street rates; the 2026 rebound reflects stabilizing rates and consolidation after a two-year slump, but 7% mortgages cap it.\n\n**Lodging and resort REITs.** RevPAR, group bookings and inbound travel; 2026's leader after five years of neglect, and the most cyclical group if higher fuel and rates eventually hit demand.\n\n**Mortgage REITs.** The yield curve, MBS spreads and the Fed's balance sheet, with GSE reform the policy wildcard; they led in 2025 on cuts and lost 12% in 2026 on hikes.\n\n**Real estate services and brokerage.** Existing-home sales near 30-year lows, mortgage rates, commission compression after the NAR settlement and consolidation; commercial brokers trade on transaction volumes, again exposed to 5% yields.\n","technology":"# Technology: where the capital went, 1999-2026\n\n*Research note for the Capital Rotation Tracker (sector id `technology`; sub-industry ids from `pipeline/taxonomy.json`). Companion data: `research/events_group3.json`, `research/regimes.json`. Prepared 7 November 2026.*\n\n## The short version\n\nTechnology is the sector the rest of the market rotates around. It fell 82% peak-to-trough in the dot-com bust, overtook financials as the largest S&P 500 sector in May 2008 and has not given that up. Three forces explain most of its moves: real interest rates (software, internet and any long-duration cash flow), its customers' capex cycle (semiconductors, memory, hardware, now the hyperscalers' AI build-out) and policy (export controls, tariffs, antitrust and the GICS reclassifications of 2018 and 2023 that moved companies between sectors).\n\n## 1999-2007: bubble, bust and rebuild\n\nThe Nasdaq rose 85.6% in 1999 and peaked at 5,048.62 on 10 March 2000 with the Fed still tightening. Between 24 March 2000 and 9 October 2002 the S&P 500 technology sector lost 82.4% and telecom 74.1% against 49.2% for the index; Cisco, briefly worth more than $500 billion, lost about 80%. Emergency cuts from 3 January 2001 could not revive capex. The Nasdaq returned 50% in 2003 on 1% fed funds, then lagged for three years (2005: +1.4% versus +4.9%) as the June 2004-June 2006 hiking cycle sent capital to energy, materials and banks. The iPhone launch on 29 June 2007 was the first tech-specific catalyst in years: Apple went from roughly $77 to $200 in a year when the S&P 500 returned 5.5%.\n\n## 2008-2012: resilience and the mobile-cloud build-out\n\nThe financial crisis was a rotation as much as a crash. Financials fell 82.6% peak-to-trough; technology fell 53%, less than the index, because its balance sheets held cash rather than mortgages. Qimonda's January 2009 insolvency marked the bottom of the DRAM cycle. With the Fed at zero and buying bonds (QE2 November 2010, QE3 September 2012), the smartphone and cloud build-out led: the Nasdaq returned 43.9% in 2009, smartphone shipments hit 725 million units in 2012 and a billion in 2013, and Apple set a record $624 billion market cap on 20 August 2012. Facebook's $38 IPO in May 2012, which halved by September, showed the market would still punish unproven monetisation.\n\n## 2013-2017: taper, consolidation, election, melt-up\n\nThe May 2013 taper tantrum hit the sector's yield-sensitive corner (telecom +11.5% in 2013 versus 32.4% for the index) but not its growth corner. Dell's $24.4 billion LBO took legacy PC hardware private. Avago-Broadcom ($37 billion, May 2015) headlined a half-year of $72.6 billion in announced semiconductor deals, six times the prior annual average, the consolidation behind the industry's later pricing power. The 2016 election was a textbook rotation: banks had their best month on record (KBE +18.7% in November 2016) while Apple and the platforms fell on trade fears. Then 2017's synchronized growth and tax cut produced the melt-up: technology +38.8%, FAANG +46.5%, telecom the worst sector at -1.3%.\n\n## 2018-2019: reclassification, trade war, Fed pivot\n\nThree things happened in 2018. GICS moved Alphabet and Facebook out of IT and Netflix and Comcast out of Consumer Discretionary into Communication Services after the close on 28 September, reclassifying roughly 10% of index market cap, which is why this taxonomy keeps an `internet_platforms` bucket that spans GICS sectors. The trade war arrived: steel tariffs in March, Section 301 tariffs from July and, in May 2019, the Huawei entity listing that cost Micron a customer worth 13% of sales and dropped the semiconductor index about 14% in a month. And the Fed's 19 December hike capped a quarter in which the Nasdaq fell 17.5% and Apple more than 31%. The pivot (first cut 31 July 2019) and the Phase One deal reversed all of it: technology returned 50.3% in 2019, almost entirely multiple expansion.\n\n## 2020-2021: pandemic winners and the speculative peak\n\nCOVID rewarded anything digital: technology +43.9% in 2020, Zoom up roughly 580% by November, PC demand +13%, Apple the first $2 trillion company on 19 August 2020. The 2021 chip shortage (15-week lead times, an estimated $210 billion of lost automaker revenue) gave semiconductors pricing power and a third straight year of outperformance at 6.3x forward sales. The speculative peak was in fintech and crypto: Coinbase listed on 14 April 2021 at a $47 billion reference valuation and closed day one at $328.28; bitcoin peaked three weeks before the Nasdaq.\n\n## 2022: the rate shock\n\nLiftoff on 16 March 2022 and eleven hikes repriced every long-duration asset. Technology returned -28.2%, communication services -39.9% and the Nasdaq -33.1% against -18.1% for the S&P 500 and +65.7% for energy. Software was roughly cut in half, Meta posted a record $230 billion one-day loss in February, Netflix fell 35% in a day in April, the F-Prime fintech index fell 72%, and Adobe's $20 billion Figma bid cost it 17% in a day. Policy added a second layer: the CHIPS Act (9 August 2022, $52.7 billion plus a 25% fab credit) pulled capital toward US fabs, and the 7 October 2022 export controls created a standing China discount on semicap names.\n\n## 2023-2025: the AI trade\n\nChatGPT (30 November 2022) and Microsoft's $10 billion OpenAI investment (23 January 2023) set the narrative; Nvidia's 24 May 2023 guidance ($11 billion versus $7.15 billion expected, stock +26%, $1 trillion within a week) made its earnings the sector bellwether. Technology returned 57.8% in 2023 and 36.6% in 2024. Hyperscaler capex went from $37.8 billion a quarter in early 2023 to $140.6 billion in Q4 2025, and that spending drove everything downstream: Super Micro into the S&P 500 (then into a governance scandal), Vertiv +252% in 2023, DRAM prices +172% in 2025, Oracle's backlog +359% on a reported $300 billion OpenAI contract. The two shocks of 2025, DeepSeek (27 January, Nvidia -17%, a record $589 billion) and Liberation Day (2 April, S&P 500 -12% in four sessions before the 9 April pause and 11 April chip exemption), were both bought; the S&P 500 made a new high on 27 June 2025 and Nvidia reached $5 trillion on 29 October 2025. Policy turned from stick to carrot: payments moved to Financials (March 2023), the GENIUS Act legalised stablecoins (July 2025), the government took 9.9% of Intel (August 2025) and the January 2026 chip tariff came with exemptions for anyone building in America. The losers were labor- and seat-based models: software peaked in September 2025 and IT services were downgraded on AI-substitution risk and hit by the $100,000 H-1B fee.\n\n## 2026: oil shock, chip melt-up, SaaS-pocalypse, credit question\n\nAnthropic's Claude Cowork plug-ins and Opus 4.6 (2-6 February) erased roughly $300 billion of software value in a day and IGV reportedly fell 24% in Q1; Claude Managed Agents (8 April) took Akamai down 17%. The Supreme Court voided the IEEPA tariffs on 20 February, a relief for import-heavy hardware and Apple. The Iran war from 28 February sent Brent to $119.50 and made energy the year's leader, yet semiconductors had their best quarter ever in Q2 (SMH +71%, SOX +106% year-to-date, Micron a $1 trillion company on 26 May) on the thesis that AI needs memory and CPUs, not just GPUs. The reversal began on 1 July (Micron -11%) and broadened: Alphabet turned free-cash-flow negative (-7% on 23 July, -13% on 29 October), Accenture had its worst day (-18%, 18 June) and best (+22%, 1 October), CoreWeave's CDS passed 1,000 bp, states imposed data-center moratoria, the Fed hiked on 16 September, Intel's PC warning dropped the SOX 7% on 30 October, and the Democratic midterm sweep on 3 November raised moratorium risk over 45% of planned capacity. XLK fell 19.2% in the month to 5 November and is up 13.2% for the year, behind energy (+40.6%); the Nasdaq's 6 October record of 27,599.89 is the cycle high for now.\n\n## What the sub-industries trade on today\n\n**Semiconductors.** Nvidia's data-center revenue and hyperscaler capex guidance, now filtered through credit: can OpenAI, CoreWeave and the neoclouds pay for what they contracted. Then export policy (the H200 case-by-case regime, the Hua Hong tool halt, Lutnick's 100% tariff threat), the consumer side (Intel's PC warning) and moratorium risk under a Democratic Congress.\n\n**Semiconductor equipment.** Leads the chip cycle by two quarters on WFE outlooks, memory capex and China. Lam draws about 35% of revenue from China, so each BIS action is a repricing event; CHIPS-funded and tariff-offset fabs and Taiwan's $250 billion pledge are the offset.\n\n**Memory and storage.** A pure price cycle: DRAM up 172% in 2025, capacity sold out through 2026 because HBM absorbed wafers. The debate is duration (Micron says through 2027, SK Hynix to 2030) versus demand destruction (PC shipments forecast -10-11% in 2026, Google's TurboQuant compression).\n\n**Application software.** Whether AI agents shrink seats or expand consumption. Each Anthropic release has been a selloff; each quarter of stable net retention a relief rally (IGV +41% from 13 April to 12 October 2026, still down over one year). Consumption pricing, take-private interest and real yields are the swing factors.\n\n**Infrastructure software and cybersecurity.** Azure and OCI growth, Oracle's single-customer backlog and BBB- rating, and the consolidation bid (Google-Wiz $32 billion, Palo Alto-CyberArk $25 billion). Security budgets are the least discretionary line in IT, but the April 2026 selloff showed edge and infrastructure names are not immune to the agent narrative.\n\n**IT services and consulting.** The most AI-exposed labor model: moat downgrades, the H-1B fee, Accenture's record down and up days, EPAM's ejection from the S&P 500. Trades at 9-12x earnings on bookings and whether AI integration work offsets shrinking billable hours.\n\n**Internet platforms and media tech.** Capex intensity versus free cash flow is the valuation axis: Alphabet lost $490 billion on 29 October 2026 despite 82% cloud growth. Antitrust appeals, LLM search disruption and the ad cycle are secondary; the sector that led 2024-25 is the worst of 2026.\n\n**Hardware, networking and components.** AI server and optical demand against memory-cost inflation (Dell's downgrade, HP's memory at 35% of bill of materials) and governance risk (Super Micro). Tariff relief helps; 2026 unit forecasts hurt.\n\n**Apple.** iPhone cycle, China, services and tariffs, with memory cost the newest input: the 25 June 2026 price increases cost 6%, yet Apple became a $5 trillion company on 28 July. Chip and smartphone exemptions and buybacks underpin the quality premium.\n\n**Fintech and payments.** Housed in Financials by GICS but tracked here: stablecoin adoption under the GENIUS Act (Circle, Coinbase), the reopened IPO window (Circle, Klarna, Figma), consumer credit, interchange regulation and the Capital One-Discover network precedent; PayPal's 2026 takeover interest shows the legacy names trade as value.\n\n**Telecom and cable.** A yield and consolidation trade: three-carrier wireless, spectrum consolidation (EchoStar's sales to AT&T and SpaceX), fiber M&A (Verizon-Frontier, AT&T-Lumen, Charter-Cox) and the bankruptcy of satellite TV. Rising long yields and the Fed hike cap the dividend argument; the sector trades on broadband share versus fixed wireless and on dividend coverage.\n","themes":"# Themes as overlays: AI, hyperscalers, data-center power, GLP-1, reshoring, cybersecurity, nuclear\n\n*Research note for the Capital Rotation Tracker (theme ids `ai`, `hyperscalers`, `datacenter_power`, `glp1`, `reshoring_infrastructure`, `cybersecurity`, `nuclear`). Themes overlap sector buckets; names also sit in their home sub-industries. Prepared 7 November 2026.*\n\n## Why overlays\n\nSector buckets say where a company is filed; themes say why its stock moved. Since 2022 the largest flows have followed cross-sector narratives rather than GICS lines: the AI trade spans semiconductors, utilities, industrials and REITs; GLP-1 moves food, devices and telehealth as well as pharma.\n\n## AI beneficiaries (from 30 November 2022)\n\nChatGPT, Microsoft's $10 billion OpenAI investment (23 January 2023) and Nvidia's $11 billion guidance (24 May 2023, stock +26%) created the theme; the Magnificent Seven rose about 76% cap-weighted in 2023. Its shocks have been about cost and financing, not demand: DeepSeek (27 January 2025, Nvidia -17%, a record $589 billion loss) questioned how much compute frontier models need; the September-October 2025 Nvidia-OpenAI ($100 billion), AMD-OpenAI (6 GW plus warrants) and Oracle ($300 billion backlog) deals raised the \"circular financing\" question; and in October-November 2026 CoreWeave's bonds fell to 78 cents, its CDS passed 1,000 bp, Gartner put more than $4 trillion of data-center assets at write-down risk and the Democratic midterm sweep raised moratorium risk. The overlay now trades on credit (neocloud and Oracle spreads, hyperscaler free cash flow) and on whether monetised AI revenue catches up with capex.\n\n## Hyperscalers / cloud capex (from 2011)\n\nCombined capex of Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft and Oracle rose from $37.8 billion in Q1 2023 to $140.6 billion in Q4 2025; 2026 guidance clusters around $730 billion. The market's rule since Amazon's October 2025 print (AWS +20.2%, stock +13%) and Alphabet's 2026 shocks (-7% in July, -13% in October) is simple: capex funded by cash flow and matched by cloud acceleration is rewarded; capex funded by debt (Alphabet's long-term debt +111% in six months, $296 billion of hyperscaler issuance in 2026) is punished. It trades on cloud growth, free cash flow, bond issuance and GPU depreciation schedules.\n\n## Data-center power and cooling (from 1 January 2023)\n\nVertiv's 252% rise in 2023, GE Vernova's April 2024 spin-off, Talen's $650 million Amazon campus sale (March 2024) and Constellation's Three Mile Island restart (20 September 2024) made power, cooling and gas turbines the industrial expression of AI. PJM capacity costs rose to more than $16 billion from $2.2 billion, which is also the theme's problem: electricity bills produced a 2026 backlash (New York's moratorium of 14 July, Chicago, Pennsylvania, New Jersey, 71% public opposition) and Goldman's estimate that 45% of planned capacity faces moratorium risk after the midterms. It trades on contracted PPAs, turbine and transformer lead times, and state politics.\n\n## GLP-1 / obesity (from 1 June 2022)\n\nWegovy's 2021 approval and the SELECT cardiovascular result (8 August 2023, Novo +18%) turned obesity drugs into a cardiometabolic franchise and a short thesis for food, devices and dialysis. The theme then became a share war: Lilly's Zepbound (November 2023) took share, Novo cut guidance on 29 July 2025 (-22% in a day, -47% for the year), the November 2025 TrumpRx deal set $245 and $50 monthly prices with a tariff exemption, and Lilly became the first $1 trillion health-care company on 21 November 2025. It trades on oral GLP-1 launches, pricing and reimbursement, compounding and telehealth volumes.\n\n## Reshoring and infrastructure (from 15 November 2021)\n\nThree laws built the theme: the IIJA (15 November 2021, $550 billion new spending), the CHIPS Act (9 August 2022, $52.7 billion plus a 25% fab credit) and the IRA (16 August 2022, $391 billion). The One Big Beautiful Bill Act (4 July 2025) split it: wind, solar and EV credits were phased out while manufacturing, nuclear and factory-depreciation incentives survived. Tariffs now do the work subsidies did: 50% metals duties, the January 2026 chip tariff with offsets for US fabs, Taiwan's $250 billion pledge and Micron's $250 billion US plan. It trades on fab and data-center construction backlogs, grid orders, tariff design after the Supreme Court ruling and whether moratoria slow the non-residential pipeline.\n\n## Cybersecurity (from 11 November 2014)\n\nSony (November 2014), Equifax (September 2017), SolarWinds (December 2020) and Colonial Pipeline (May 2021, Executive Order 14028) made security a non-discretionary budget line and a policy mandate. The 19 July 2024 CrowdStrike outage (8.5 million devices, stock -11%) moved share between platforms without cutting budgets, and 2025's consolidation (Google-Wiz $32 billion, Palo Alto-CyberArk $25 billion) set takeover premiums. It trades on platform consolidation, federal spending, breach headlines and, since April 2026, whether AI agents expand the attack surface or compress seat-based security SaaS.\n\n## Nuclear renaissance (from 1 June 2023)\n\nFukushima (11 March 2011) halved uranium prices and began a decade-long bear market; the turn came from hyperscalers: Three Mile Island (September 2024), the Google-Kairos and Amazon-X-energy SMR deals (October 2024), Trump's 23 May 2025 executive orders (10 large reactors under construction by 2030), the $80 billion Westinghouse program (28 October 2025), X-energy's record $1 billion IPO (24 April 2026) and Westinghouse's S-1 (31 July 2026). It trades on uranium and enrichment prices, DOE and NRC milestones, hyperscaler PPAs and the same data-center politics as the power theme; existing fleets carry the cash flow, SMR developers the option value.\n"};